    },
    {
      "id": 20,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "7. Derived panels — read these with the two headline metrics",
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 55
      },
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 21,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "DB wall-clock vs DynamoDB's own clock",
      "description": "db_wall_avg_by_route is wall-clock time around the SDK await, which absorbs event-loop queueing (measured 12.1x inflation with the database unchanged). cloudwatch_srl_by_operation is DynamoDB's own reported clock, scraped by the collector. The gap between the two lines is the queueing signal, not extra DB time — compare against the \"Queueing delay by route\" panel. TIME SKEW: the two sides of this comparison do not describe the same instant. The service histogram arrives via OTLP within seconds; DynamoDB's clock is scraped by the collector from CloudWatch and lands about 2 minutes later (measured 2026-09-01 -- the forwarded series is the native one shifted +2 min, values preserved exactly). At steady state the subtrahend is flat and the error is hundredths of a millisecond; the largest excursion observed was 7.4 ms against a 642-938 ms signal, about 1%. Read a live run knowing the DynamoDB line lags.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 6,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 56
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
          },
          "expr": ${jsonencode(db_wall_avg_by_route)}
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
          },
          "expr": ${jsonencode(cloudwatch_srl_by_operation)}
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "pointSize": 5,
            "spanNulls": true
          },
          "unit": "ms"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 22,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Queueing delay by route",
      "description": "db_wall_avg_by_route minus DynamoDB's own reported clock for the operations THAT route issues (slo.yaml attribution.operations: /items/:pk/:sk → GetItem, /items → PutItem, /feeds/:pk → Query, /reports → Query + PutItem). Not a table-wide average — averaging unlike operations together, BatchWriteItem from seeding included, subtracted ~1.8 ms too little from /reports. A positive, growing gap is time spent inside the process around the await, and that includes AWS SDK retry backoff on throttled DynamoDB calls — so while throttle events are non-zero it is not evidence about the service. Shows NaN for any route with no traffic in the window — it is a 0/0 average, not a broken panel. At idle, most routes read NaN because the heartbeat touches each route only once a minute; the value resolves once real load is running. TIME SKEW: the two sides of this comparison do not describe the same instant. The service histogram arrives via OTLP within seconds; DynamoDB's clock is scraped by the collector from CloudWatch and lands about 2 minutes later (measured 2026-09-01 -- the forwarded series is the native one shifted +2 min, values preserved exactly). At steady state the subtrahend is flat and the error is hundredths of a millisecond; the largest excursion observed was 7.4 ms against a 642-938 ms signal, about 1%. Read a live run knowing the DynamoDB line lags.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 6,
        "x": 6,
        "y": 56
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
          },
          "expr": ${jsonencode(queueing_ms_by_route)}
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "pointSize": 5,
            "spanNulls": true
          },
          "unit": "ms"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 23,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "CPU saturation",
      "description": "CPU seconds burned per second (by task), over the 0.25 vCPU Fargate ceiling. 1.0 is the ceiling — a task pinned at or above it is CPU-bound regardless of what the DB clock says. READ IT ONE WAY ONLY: that is sufficient evidence of CPU-bound, never necessary. http.server.cpu.duration sums only the two bracketed synchronous blocks in src/handlers.js (feed's summarize, report's pbkdf2); JSON serialisation, AWS SDK work and the OTel export are uncounted, and getItem/putItem bracket nothing so they contribute no series at all. A task can be genuinely pinned while this panel reads 0.2. The converse does not follow either: on 2026-09-01 event-loop utilisation read 1.000 while CPU sat at 3–16%, and that was AWS SDK retry backoff on throttled DynamoDB calls, not work — a high event-loop number with low CPU is not evidence the service was the constraint.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 6,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 56
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
          },
          "expr": ${jsonencode(cpu_saturation_ratio)}
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "pointSize": 5,
            "spanNulls": true
          },
          "unit": "percentunit"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 24,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Event-loop delay p99 by task",
      "description": "nodejs_eventloop_delay_p99_seconds, by (instance) — one line per task; four lines once Task 20 brings autoscaling to 4 tasks. A rising delay here with CPU saturation flat says only that the loop had pending work the CPU accounting does not see; it does not say what kind. On 2026-09-01 it reached 610 ms while CPU sat at 3–16%, and the cause was AWS SDK retry backoff on throttled DynamoDB calls — pending timers, which are neither blocking work nor raw compute. Read it beside the throttle-events panel.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 6,
        "x": 18,
        "y": 56
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${prometheus_datasource_uid}"
          },
          "expr": ${jsonencode(eventloop_delay_p99)}
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "pointSize": 5,
            "spanNulls": true
          },
          "unit": "s"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "displayMode": "table",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull",
            "max"
          ]
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
